1. Code Quality & Readability

✅ Code structure

Component files are small, focused, and meaningful.

Each component handles a single responsibility.

Code is modular and reusable.

Follows project folder conventions (components/, hooks/, utils/, etc.).

✅ Readability

Variable and function names are descriptive and consistent (camelCase for JS, PascalCase for components).

Avoids deep nesting or complex conditional logic — use early returns.

No unnecessary console logs or commented-out code.

Follows ESLint & Prettier rules.

✅ Consistency

Code follows the team’s style guide (e.g., Airbnb or custom lint rules).

Proper use of semicolons, indentation, and spacing.

⚛️ 2. React-Specific Checks

✅ Component best practices

Functional components (with hooks) are preferred over class components.

Avoids unnecessary re-renders (use React.memo, useCallback, useMemo appropriately).

Uses prop destructuring at the top of the component.

Default values and prop types (or TypeScript interfaces) are defined.

✅ Hooks

Hooks follow the rules of hooks (only called at top level, not inside loops or conditionals).

useEffect dependencies are correct and minimal (no infinite loops).

Cleanup functions are used in effects where necessary.

Custom hooks are abstracted properly and reusable.

✅ State Management

State is minimal and localized; derived data isn’t duplicated in state.

Context or external state management (Redux, Zustand, etc.) is used only when necessary.

💄 3. UI & UX Review

✅ Design consistency

UI matches Figma / design specs.

Uses consistent spacing, color, and typography tokens.

Responsive behavior verified (mobile, tablet, desktop).

✅ Accessibility (a11y)

Semantic HTML tags (button, label, nav, etc.) used correctly.

ARIA attributes where needed.

Keyboard navigation and focus states verified.

Images have alt text.

✅ User Interaction

Buttons, inputs, and links have appropriate feedback states.

Form validations and error messages are clear.

No layout shifts or flickers on interaction.

⚙️ 4. Performance & Optimization

✅ Rendering

Avoids heavy computations inside render — use useMemo.

Conditional rendering doesn’t cause UI flicker.

List rendering uses key props correctly and uniquely.

✅ Network & Data

API calls are debounced/throttled if needed.

Fetch calls are canceled on component unmount when appropriate.

Data fetching handled via hooks (React Query, SWR, etc.).

✅ Code Splitting

Lazy loading and suspense are used for large modules.

Unused imports or libraries removed.

🔒 5. Security

✅ Data handling

No hardcoded secrets or tokens.

Input values sanitized before use.

Avoids direct DOM manipulation (dangerouslySetInnerHTML used safely).

✅ API

All API endpoints are validated and error-handled.

Tokens or headers not exposed in logs.

🧪 6. Testing & Error Handling

✅ Testing

Unit tests exist for critical logic or hooks.

Component rendering tests cover main UI flows.

Edge cases considered.

✅ Error Handling

Try/catch used around API calls.

Fallback UI or boundaries in place (ErrorBoundary).

Graceful loading and empty states handled.

🚀 7. Deployment & Maintainability

✅ Build readiness

No unused dependencies or console warnings.

Environment variables correctly set up.

.env not committed.

✅ Maintainability

Comments only where logic isn’t self-explanatory.

Proper folder naming and grouping (components, hooks, assets, etc.).

Type safety ensured (if TypeScript).
